I think a better explanation for these nations not choosing a tribal
language for an official language of the nation is that official
languages of a nation are the languages of a dominating force -- one
with enough local power say to define by fiat what the official
language will be. (Hopefully you won't quibble with me adding "of the
nation"; I intended it as a useful clarifying addition, not for use in
twisting meanings.) I venture that it's rare or non-existent for the
language of a non-dominant group -- even if it is the language of a
numerical majority -- to become a nation's official language. Now,
European languages often *stay* official after a European power no
longer dominates a country, but that's a different matter.

Thus with no tribe in these countries having had enough power to
dominate the country, there was no official language until English
(French, etc.), and after the Europeans were gone, there was not enough
reason to switch back (inertia and all that).

Keep in mind as well that the modern African nations do not
necesssarily have any relation to the Africans' ideas -- if any -- of
natural divisions of areas of land that may have been pointlessly huge
from their standpoint in pre-European times...

Just food for thought.

